WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
Programs that create child processes must also manage child lifecycle.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.480
On POSIX-style systems, child status notifications can inform the parent that a child has changed state.

3
00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:17.500
That notification is useful, but it should not turn the signal handler into a full process-management routine.

4
00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.460
A defensive design keeps child notification handling light.

5
00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:27.960
The handler can request that normal code check child status, or it can notify an event loop through a safe mechanism.

6
00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:38.300
Mainline code can then perform waits, update internal state, restart planned work if appropriate, release resources, and write logs using ordinary program logic.

7
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:43.780
Avoiding unreaped child processes is a maintainability and operations concern.

8
00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:53.080
A parent that starts children should have a clear plan for tracking them, observing completion, handling errors, and cleaning up associated resources.

9
00:00:53.180 --> 00:00:57.660
This plan should work during normal operation and during shutdown.

10
00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:02.580
Coordination with event loops, worker pools, or supervisors should be explicit.

11
00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:10.440
Reviewers should ask where children are created, where status is collected, what happens during shutdown, and how timeouts or failed starts are reported.

12
00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:16.380
Predictable lifecycle management reduces surprises without putting unsafe work in the handler.
